/* ---- v4.95 lobby stage guard ----
 * The lobby stage is the map entry's +0x80 level (0xdec97a4, stride 0x98, sid at +0x88), switched by the native loader
 * 0x743420 (mode 1/2: unwant the old stage from the level manager's second want list 0x1afcf38/0x1afcf40, copy the new
 * name to NetGameManager+0x74b8, LevelWantLoad {hash, sid, flags 3}, show) and read by the lobby camera 0x5b4e60 (which
 * only aims at menu-spawner-1 once that level is in state 8). The converted PS3 stages are 6-32 MB against 9 MB retail.
 * Level states (level +0x30, Level::Update 0x10f3500): 8 loaded, 0xc error (a package failed: missing file, wrong format,
 * no loading-heap page). Packages (level +0x80, count +0x78): status +0x20 (0xc logged in), header copy +0x238 (version
 * 0xa79), page count +0x248. Package pages come from g_loadingHeap 0x16e7bb8 (LoadingHeap::Init 0xdcf030 from the memory
 * config 0x16f8d50: ALLOCATION_LOADING_HEAP 0x10800000 = 132 pages of 2 MB, LOADING_HEAP_HIDDEN 0x600000 = 3 pages; +0x28
 * pages total, +0x38 pages used, +0xf4 peak; page size 0x18bed90, normal limit 0x18bed94). The package manager 0xdd30d0
 * waits in state 7 until (free pages) >= pages + 10, but starts anyway when nothing is waiting to unload, and an empty
 * heap then ends in LoadingHeap::Alloc 0xdcf3b0's "Out of memory" assert (int 0x41). Resident VRAM (textures) is staged
 * through the same pages (max 32 at a time) and copied into kAllocIoTexture (ALLOCATION_TEXTURE 1260 MB).
 * Safety net (no PS3 counterpart: PS3 shipped only stages that fit): while the current stage is wanted, if its level
 * reports 0xc, or its package header says it cannot get its pages, or it is not loaded after 20 s, hide and unwant it,
 * remember it, point every map entry that names it at the retail san-upper-multi-stage (so 0x743420 does not request it
 * again) and switch the lobby to that stage the way 0x743420 mode 1 does. */
static const char stage_fallback[]="san-upper-multi-stage";
#define STAGE_FALLBACK_SID 0x703ab0f4u
/* plausible heap/data pointer: canonical user address, 8-byte aligned (the guard never follows anything else) */
