/* v5.26 script-instance teardown watch (PS4 0x1257010 = ScriptManager stop-instance; rdi = instance). It allocates
 * count*8 bytes, count = u32 instance+0x644 (entries of the wait lists at +0x658/+0x668), unless instance+0x640 bit0.
 * Co-op Hunter post-match stopped an instance whose count was garbage (0xffff8a24) -> allocation assert.
 * v5.31: v5.33d showed the first teardown at solo co-op Arena start (0.9 s into Playing) on such an instance: the script
 * process (vtable 0x16bfbc0, 0x738 bytes, ctor 0x1258710, instance at +0xb0, dtor 0x12583f0 -> 0x1257010) was at 0xb3fff50
 * and the page from 0xb400000 on was no longer writable and held foreign data. The ctor writes +0xb0, so the process
 * memory was released or remapped while the process was still in the process tree (process-manager delete pass
 * 0x11a6910 -> 0x11a6560 -> vt+0x10). The v5.26 guard then faulted writing +0x644.
 * Now a value stub: the helper gets the saved registers (rdi = instance, [10] = return address) and only READS.
 * Bad = count > 0x10000, or wait-list pointers +0x658/+0x668 not sane while count != 0. Bad instances are recorded:
 * sg[1] count, sgd[0..46] dump of the first one (as v5.26), sgd[48..55] ring of the last 4 {instance, return address},
 * sgo[0..23] the owning process (instance - 0xb0 when called from the process dtors 0x125840f/0x125843f) +0x00..+0xb8 of
 * the first one. U3MP_SCRIPT_GUARD=1 (RS2 bit 0x40000) makes the stub return without running the teardown (no writes),
 * counted in sgd[47]. */
static int sg_ptr(uint64_t p){return p>0x100000&&p<0x7fffffffffffull&&!(p&7);}
/* v5.32: v5.34 showed the bad instance is the stack instance of the state-script "on" runner 0x1240590 (= PS3 f_9d9xxx
 * caller; ctor 0x1255e20 at rbp-0x6b0, run 0x12564b0, teardown 0x12407f1). 0x12564b0 stores the track group's int16 track
 * count (group +0) into instance+0x644 BEFORE its 64-track limit check ("Too many tracks"), as PS3 does (lhz 0(r25) ->
 * +1080, then cmplwi 64), so a track group pointer that does not point at a track group (count 0x8a24) reaches the
 * teardown with a garbage count on PS3 too: bad data, not a code difference. Capture which group/script it is:
 * sgo[0..3] return chain above 0x1240590 (rbp walk), [4] track group (instance+0x60), [5..7] group +0..+0x10,
 * [8..12] group name (group +0x10, 40 chars), [13] runner rdi (rbp-0x680), [14..19] its +0..+0x28, [20..23] instance
 * +0x620..+0x638. */
