/* ===== agent B b025: UI image quads with a 2-texture material get the material's own default for the missing texture =====
 * TEST (dev 47d60282 + batch51): in Profile > Emblem the c014 guard (0xe66a67) fires every frame for the image-picker cells:
 * handle 1 = "emblems/<icon>", handle 2 = 0, so the guard's transparent stand-in makes every cell blank.
 * Why: the picker cell is a type-2 value (PS4 0x76a5a9 = PS3 f_33b1e8: f_27e47c handle at +0x10, colour +0x2c; PS3 sets
 * the same fields, so the datasource is already 1:1). PS4's UI quad 0x63dc40 picks the UI material by the texture format:
 * DXT1 (0x47) -> table entry 0xc, else 0xb (UI material table ctx+0x68, filled at 0x63bdc4..0x63c06f by name:
 * 0xb = "general/hud/alpha-icon:default", 0xc = "general/hud/alpha-icon24:default"; PS3 1.19 loads the same two names
 * into the same slots at 0x25b6e8 / 0x25b720). In hud.pak the material alpha-icon24 (hud/textured-2d.fx, ND_TRANSPARENCY)
 * declares two textures, g_tColorMap (default default-color-white) and g_tTransparencyMap (default default-trans), while
 * alpha-icon declares one (ND_TRANSPARENCY2). The PS3 emblem icons are all DXT1 (batch51), so they take alpha-icon24, and
 * the quad supplies only the colour map: 0x63dc40 builds its batch as {material, handle, 0, 0} (0x63dcab..0x63dcbb), the
 * executor 0xe41a60 copies the handles into a 3-entry texture array (0xe41c69..0xe41d0c, absent ones stay 0) and the
 * shader reads two. The ND material system binds an instance's textures over the material's default texture set, which
 * is what the unbound transparency slot gets; PS4's own UI code does the same for its type-8 material quads (0x63ca10
 * takes the textures from the material's texture collection, GetNumTexturesByIndex / GetTextureByIndex, as 0x6405c0
 * reads them: entry +0x18 collection count, +0x20 16-byte collections {u32 technique, u32 count, textures*}, texture
 * entry {p0, p1} -> p0 ? *p0 : *p1 ... ). The executor lost that fallback, so a slot without a handle is 0.
 * Port: at 0xe41d19 (the array is complete, rax = its address when handle 1 was valid) every entry still 0 below the
 * material's technique-0 texture count is filled with that material texture (the default-trans texture for the emblem
 * icons), with the same type-2 fix-up the executor applies to handle textures (0xe41ca4). Entries with a handle are never
 * touched. c014's guard stays as the last safety net. Switch U3MP_UI_MATDEF=0: stub left out.
 * eb[216] arrays seen, [217] entries filled, [218] refused (no material / collection), [219] last material entry (low).
 * agent B b028 (rework after TEST2's Profile > Emblem fault 0xe66b0d with b025 on; eb217 = 0 in that run, so no fill was
 * involved): the resource writer 0xe66940 reads texture[i] at 0xe66a67 (c014's guard replaces only 0) and then
 * 0xe66a99: type = [tex] & 3; type 2/3 -> desc = [tex+8], desc 0 -> rbx = 0; type 1 -> rbx = [tex+8]; type 0 -> rbx = 0;
 * 0xe66b0d: mov rdx,[rbx] (the fault). A texture object is readable there iff its type is not 0 and [tex+8] is
 * non-zero (k28_ok). The writer reads this same array in the same call chain (0xe41d54 -> 0xe90150 -> 0xe901c1 ->
 * 0xe90055 -> 0xe66940, TEST's stack), so: (1) a material default is used only if k28_ok, else the entry stays 0 for the
 * guard; (2) an entry the packet's own handle filled with an object the writer would fault on is cleared to 0, so the
 * guard's transparent stand-in replaces it (not PS3 behaviour: a guard like c014's, counted). eb[237] entries cleared,
 * [238] its first dword, [239] [tex+8] low, [240] slot | shader low byte << 8, [241] / [242] the handle's name chars
 * (+0x20 / +0x28, as c014's ec[153..154]). Default stays off (main): U3MP_UI_MATDEF=1 turns it on. */
